---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';

const pageTitle = "How to Convert a Website to a Windows Desktop App (EXE)";
const pageDescription = "Step-by-step: turn a website into a Windows EXE with SiteToApp. Pick Electron or Tauri, choose NSIS or portable, build, and distribute. Free and no-code.";

const breadcrumb = {
  "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
  "itemListElement": [
    { "@type": "ListItem", "position": 1, "name": "Home", "item": "https://sitetoapp.dev/" },
    { "@type": "ListItem", "position": 2, "name": "Guides", "item": "https://sitetoapp.dev/guides" },
    { "@type": "ListItem", "position": 3, "name": "How to Convert a Website to a Windows Desktop App (EXE)" }
  ]
};

const desktopGuideSchema = { "@graph": [{
  "@type": "Article",
  "headline": "How to Convert a Website to a Windows Desktop App (EXE)",
  "author": { "@type": "Organization", "@id": "https://sitetoapp.dev/#org", "name": "SiteToApp" },
  "publisher": { "@type": "Organization", "@id": "https://sitetoapp.dev/#org", "name": "SiteToApp", "logo": { "@type": "ImageObject", "url": "https://sitetoapp.dev/favicon.png" } },
  "description": "Turn any website into a standalone Windows desktop application: add your URL, pick the Electron or Tauri engine, choose an NSIS installer or portable EXE, and distribute it.",
  "image": "https://sitetoapp.dev/og-image.jpg",
  "datePublished": "2026-11-09",
  "url": "https://sitetoapp.dev/guides/website-to-desktop-windows",
  "mainEntityOfPage": "https://sitetoapp.dev/guides/website-to-desktop-windows",
  "about": { "@type": "Thing", "name": "Website to Windows EXE converter" }
}, breadcrumb]};
---

<Layout title={pageTitle} description={pageDescription} bodyClass="landing-page" structuredData={desktopGuideSchema}>
  <main class="shell">
    <article class="content-panel">
      <h1>How to Convert a Website to a Windows Desktop App (EXE)</h1>
      <p>Not every user is on a phone. If your audience works on Windows, a desktop wrapper gives your website a native icon in the taskbar, a proper install wizard, and shell-level behavior — all without maintaining a second codebase. Here's how to do it with the <a href="/windows">free SiteToApp Windows builder</a>, from first config to distributing the EXE.</p>

      <h2>Step 1 — Add your URL and configure the app</h2>
      <p>Open the <a href="/windows">Windows builder</a> and enter your website URL and app name. The builder automatically scrapes your site's favicon for the app icon — or upload your own 512×512 PNG for a sharper result. Set the window dimensions: <strong>1200×800</strong> is a solid default for most content sites, while <strong>1440×900</strong> suits dashboards or admin panels that benefit from more horizontal space.</p>

      <h2>Step 2 — Pick a build engine</h2>
      <p>This is the most important decision. Two engines are available:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Electron (~65–90 MB)</strong> — bundles a full Chromium + Node.js runtime with your app. Everything works out of the box: file system access, background workers, auto-updates, and a huge plugin ecosystem. The trade-off is a larger install size and slightly slower cold start while Chromium boots. Choose Electron if your site uses Node.js features, or if you want maximum browser compatibility.</li>
        <li><strong>Tauri (~5–10 MB)</strong> — a Rust shell that reuses the system's WebView2 engine (built into Windows 10/11). The result is a dramatically smaller installer and near-instant cold start. Choose Tauri when size and speed are your top priorities and you don't need Node.js.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Not sure which to pick? Read the full <a href="/guides/webview-vs-electron-vs-tauri">WebView vs Electron vs Tauri comparison</a>.</p>

      <h2>Step 3 — Choose your package format</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>NSIS installer</strong> — a standard Windows setup wizard with a Start Menu shortcut, desktop icon, and uninstaller. Best choice for apps you're distributing to customers or non-technical users.</li>
        <li><strong>Portable EXE</strong> — a single self-contained executable that runs from anywhere, requires no installation, and works from a USB drive. Perfect for internal tools, kiosk setups, or power users who prefer clean systems.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Step 4 — Build and download</h2>
      <p>Click <em>Build → EXE</em>. The build runs on GitHub Actions (<code>windows-latest</code> runner) and completes in about a minute. You get a direct download link for the installer or portable EXE, plus the full Electron or Tauri source workspace with a pre-wired GitHub Actions workflow — so rebuilding for future versions costs nothing.</p>

      <h2>Step 5 — Distribute your EXE</h2>
      <p>You have several options:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Host on your website</strong> — a simple download link, the most direct path to your existing audience.</li>
        <li><strong>GitHub Releases</strong> — attach the EXE to a versioned release for a free, reliable CDN with a changelog.</li>
        <li><strong>Microsoft Store</strong> — package as MSIX for the Store. See <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/windows/msix/overview" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Microsoft's MSIX documentation</a> for packaging steps.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Handling the Windows SmartScreen warning</h2>
      <p>If Windows SmartScreen flags your installer as "unrecognized", it's because the EXE is unsigned. Users can click <strong>More info → Run anyway</strong> to proceed — the app is a clean wrapper around your website with no malware or trackers (see the <a href="/privacy">privacy policy</a>). This warning disappears once the executable accumulates enough reputation, or you can code-sign it with a certificate from a trusted CA.</p>

      <h2>When a desktop app beats a browser tab</h2>
      <p>Desktop wrappers shine for internal dashboards, POS systems, kiosk displays, and any tool users open dozens of times daily. They give your site a pinned taskbar icon, preserve window state between sessions, and can tap native OS notifications and file system access. And just like the mobile wrappers, content updates on your site appear instantly in the app — no rebuild needed.</p>

      <p>Ready to get started? <a href="/windows"><strong>Open the free Windows EXE builder</strong></a>, or check the <a href="/faq">FAQ</a> for SmartScreen, signing, and sizing questions.</p>
    </article>
  </main>
</Layout>
